topic("para", "Coccidia and Coccidiosis: Eimeria and Isospora in Domestic Animals and Poultry", {
  o: ["Describe the structure and life cycle of Eimeria", "Describe coccidiosis of poultry, cattle, sheep, goats, pigs, dogs and rabbits", "Describe species specificity and site specificity", "Describe pathogenesis, diagnosis and control", "Describe immunity and vaccines"],
  i: "Coccidiosis, caused by Eimeria and Isospora, is among the most common and costly protozoal diseases in poultry and in young ruminants. The syllabus lists coccidia and coccidiosis as a topic of its own, and then cyst-forming coccidia (Toxoplasma, Sarcocystis, Neospora) and Cryptosporidium as separate topics.",
  s: "A single oocyst swallowed by a bird turns into hundreds of thousands of new oocysts within a week, because the parasite multiplies inside the cells lining the gut and breaks them open. That is why the disease in a crowded poultry house or calf shed can explode so quickly.",
  n: [
    ["Classification and general features", "Phylum Apicomplexa, class Sporozoea, subclass Coccidia, order Eucoccidiorida. Family Eimeriidae: Eimeria (oocyst with four sporocysts, each with two sporozoites), Isospora (two sporocysts, each with four sporozoites), Cyclospora; family Cryptosporidiidae: Cryptosporidium. Coccidia are intracellular parasites of the intestinal epithelium (and liver, kidney in some species). They are host specific: Eimeria species of one host do not infect another, and each species infects a particular site of the gut. About 7 species infect chickens, 11 cattle, 11 sheep, 10 goats, 4 species in pigs, and 12 in rabbits."],
    ["Life cycle of Eimeria", "Monoxenous, with three phases. Sporogony (exogenous): unsporulated oocysts are shed in faeces, and sporulate in 1 to 3 days in the presence of oxygen, moisture and warmth, forming sporulated oocysts with four sporocysts each containing two sporozoites. Infection: the host swallows sporulated oocysts; sporozoites are released in the gut by enzymes and bile, penetrate epithelial cells, and become trophozoites. Schizogony (merogony): a trophozoite divides into a schizont with many merozoites; the cell breaks, and merozoites invade fresh cells (2 to 4 generations, number depends on species). Gametogony: merozoites become male microgametes and female macrogametes; the zygote forms an oocyst wall and is shed unsporulated. The prepatent period is 4 to 14 days, shortest in chickens. Oocysts are very resistant to the environment and to most disinfectants."],
    ["Pathogenesis of coccidiosis", "Damage arises from the destruction of epithelial cells by the schizonts and gametocytes. Large numbers of schizonts in the crypts cause bleeding, loss of mucosa, fluid and protein loss and secondary bacterial invasion. The most pathogenic species have large schizonts in deeper layers. Stress, overcrowding, wet litter, poor hygiene, malnutrition and concurrent disease intensify outbreaks. Immunity is strong but species specific and develops after exposure; young animals are the most susceptible. Clinical signs of heavy infection: diarrhoea (bloody in some), dehydration, weight loss, anaemia and death. Subclinical coccidiosis reduces growth and feed conversion."],
    ["Poultry coccidiosis", "Eimeria tenella (caecum; acute haemorrhagic caecal coccidiosis with bloody droppings, huddling, ruffled feathers; death in 4 to 6 days in young chicks), E. necatrix (middle small intestine; ballooning and haemorrhage), E. acervulina (duodenum; white transverse striations, reduced growth), E. maxima (mid intestine; thickened wall with orange mucus), E. brunetti (lower intestine and rectum; necrotic enteritis-like lesions), E. mitis, E. praecox. Lesion scoring is used to diagnose and evaluate control. In broilers, 3 to 6 week old birds are most at risk, and in layers and growers at 8 to 12 weeks. Mixed infections are common."],
    ["Coccidiosis in cattle and buffalo", "Eimeria bovis and E. zuernii (the most pathogenic; caecum and colon) and E. auburnensis, E. ellipsoidalis and others. Calves of 3 weeks to 6 months are susceptible, particularly after weaning, transport or in crowded pens. Signs: diarrhoea with blood and mucus, tenesmus, dehydration, weight loss, and sometimes nervous signs in the so-called 'nervous coccidiosis' (with tremors, convulsions and recumbency). Immunity develops after exposure; adults act as carriers."],
    ["Coccidiosis in sheep, goats, pigs, dogs, cats and rabbits", "Sheep: E. ovinoidalis (caecum and colon, the most pathogenic) and E. crandallis in lambs, with watery diarrhoea, weight loss and death; goats: E. arloingi, E. ninakohlyakimovae, with severe diarrhoea in kids. Pigs: Isospora suis in piglets of 1 to 3 weeks (yellowish pasty diarrhoea, poor growth), Eimeria debliecki and E. scabra in older pigs. Dogs and cats: Isospora canis, I. ohioensis, I. felis, I. rivolta (mostly mild, diarrhoea in puppies and kittens). Rabbits: hepatic coccidiosis due to Eimeria stiedae (bile ducts, liver lesions with white nodules) and intestinal coccidiosis by E. magna, E. irresidua and others."],
    ["Diagnosis", "Faecal flotation to detect oocysts (but oocysts may be absent in the acute disease, in which the damage is done in the prepatent period; heavy infection and counts above a threshold are significant, as many animals carry oocysts without disease). Identification of species by oocyst size, shape, colour, micropyle, sporulation time, and the site and appearance of lesions. Post-mortem examination of the intestine for lesions, smears of mucosal scrapings to see schizonts and gametocytes. Lesion scoring in poultry. Differentiation of coccidiosis from other causes of bloody diarrhoea (salmonellosis, necrotic enteritis, helminths)."],
    ["Control", "Management: clean dry litter, avoid overcrowding, clean feeders and drinkers, remove faeces, separate age groups, all-in all-out systems, control of flies and rodents, and rapid removal of sick animals. Chemoprophylaxis and therapy by anticoccidial drugs in feed or water (types and doses in pharmacology) with rotation or shuttle programmes to avoid resistance. Vaccination: live vaccines containing virulent or attenuated (precocious) lines of Eimeria for poultry (and some for lambs). Immunity builds by low-level exposure. Anticoccidial resistance is widespread and is one reason for vaccine use."],
    ["Oocyst structure and identification features", "Oocyst features include size, shape (ovoid, ellipsoid, spherical), the colour of the wall, the presence or absence of a micropyle (a small opening at the pole) and polar cap, residuum, and sporocyst arrangement. Eimeria has four sporocysts with two sporozoites each; Isospora has two with four each. Oocysts must be sporulated (in 2.5 per cent potassium dichromate solution) for identification."]
  ],
  tb: { h: ["Host", "Main pathogenic species", "Site", "Notable feature"], r: [["Chicken", "E. tenella", "Caecum", "Bloody caecal coccidiosis"], ["Chicken", "E. necatrix, E. maxima, E. acervulina", "Small intestine", "Ballooning, thickened wall, white striae"], ["Cattle", "E. bovis, E. zuernii", "Caecum, colon", "Bloody diarrhoea, nervous coccidiosis"], ["Sheep, goat", "E. ovinoidalis, E. arloingi", "Caecum, colon", "Diarrhoea in lambs, kids"], ["Pig", "Isospora suis", "Small intestine", "Piglet diarrhoea"], ["Rabbit", "E. stiedae", "Bile ducts", "Hepatic coccidiosis"], ["Dog, cat", "Isospora canis, I. felis", "Small intestine", "Mild, diarrhoea in pups"]] },
  df: ["Coccidiosis: disease caused by Eimeria or Isospora in the epithelial cells of the gut.", "Sporulation: development of sporozoites within the oocyst in the environment.", "Schizogony: asexual multiplication in host cells producing merozoites."],
  tm: [["Oocyst", "Resistant egg-like stage"], ["Sporocyst", "Sac within the oocyst holding sporozoites"], ["Micropyle", "Opening in the oocyst wall"], ["Schizont", "Multinucleate stage producing merozoites"], ["Microgamete", "Male gamete"], ["Macrogamete", "Female gamete"], ["Lesion scoring", "Grading of intestinal lesions"], ["Precocious line", "Early-maturing attenuated Eimeria for vaccines"]],
  st: ["Sporulated oocysts are swallowed.", "Sporozoites invade gut cells.", "Schizogony produces merozoites.", "Gametogony gives oocysts.", "Unsporulated oocysts leave in faeces.", "Oocysts sporulate in the environment."],
  ex: ["A 4-week-old broiler flock with bloody droppings.", "Calves after weaning and transport show bloody diarrhoea.", "A litter of piglets at 10 days with yellow diarrhoea."],
  k: ["Life cycle phases.", "Species specificity.", "Poultry species and lesions.", "Diagnosis problems.", "Control by management, drugs, vaccines."],
  m: ["Oocysts are not always present in clinical coccidiosis. Eimeria of one host does not infect another. Oocysts need oxygen and moisture to sporulate."],
  sa: [["Describe the structure of a sporulated Eimeria oocyst.", "Four sporocysts, each with two sporozoites."], ["Name the most pathogenic species in poultry.", "Eimeria tenella (caecum) and E. necatrix."], ["Why are coccidia host specific?", "Because each species is adapted to the cells and immune environment of its own host."]],
  la: [["Describe the life cycle of Eimeria with special reference to poultry coccidiosis.", "Sporogony, schizogony, gametogony, species, lesions, diagnosis, control."], ["Write an account of coccidiosis in cattle, sheep and pigs.", "Species, signs, epidemiology, control."]],
  q: [["A sporulated Eimeria oocyst has", ["Four sporocysts with two sporozoites each", "Two sporocysts with four sporozoites each", "No sporocysts", "One sporocyst"], 0, "Isospora has the reverse."],
    ["Bloody caecal coccidiosis in chickens is due to", ["Eimeria tenella", "E. acervulina", "E. mitis", "E. praecox"], 0, "Caecum."],
    ["Coccidia are", ["Host specific", "Not host specific", "Extracellular", "Free-living"], 0, "Species barriers."],
    ["Isospora suis affects", ["Piglets", "Calves", "Lambs", "Chicks"], 0, "Neonatal."],
    ["Eimeria stiedae lives in the", ["Bile ducts of rabbits", "Caecum of fowl", "Lung", "Skin"], 0, "Hepatic coccidiosis."]],
  f: [["Eimeria", "Four sporocysts"], ["Isospora", "Two sporocysts"], ["Oocyst", "Resistant stage"], ["Schizogony", "Asexual multiplication"], ["Anticoccidial", "Drug against coccidia"]],
  d: ["Draw the Eimeria life cycle.", "Practise poultry lesion scoring on pictures.", "Plan litter management for a broiler shed."],
});
/* ================= PARASITOLOGY protozoa part 2 and practical ================= */
