topic("micro", "Clostridium: Tetanus, Botulism, Blackleg, Enterotoxaemia, Malignant Oedema and Black Disease", {
  o: ["Describe the general characters of Clostridium", "Describe C. tetani, C. botulinum and their toxins", "Describe C. chauvoei, C. septicum, C. novyi and C. perfringens types A to E", "Describe diagnosis and anaerobic culture", "Describe vaccination and control programmes"],
  i: "Clostridia are Gram positive, spore-forming anaerobes that cause some of the commonest and deadliest diseases of livestock: tetanus, botulism, blackleg, malignant oedema, black disease, enterotoxaemia (pulpy kidney, struck, lamb dysentery) and gas gangrene. The syllabus lists Clostridium with the toxins of each species.",
  s: "Clostridium lives in soil and in the gut of animals as a spore. It only becomes dangerous when oxygen is low and conditions change: a dead tissue in a wound, a sudden change of feed, a damaged liver. Then it releases potent toxins that do the killing.",
  n: [
    ["General characters", "Large Gram positive rods (0.5 to 2 by 3 to 8 micrometres), spore-forming (spores oval or round, central, subterminal or terminal, bulging the cell), strictly anaerobic (a few tolerate air), many motile with peritrichous flagella (C. perfringens is non-motile), catalase negative. Cultured on blood agar anaerobically, in cooked meat medium (saccharolytic species turn it red, proteolytic species digest it and blacken it) and in thioglycollate broth. Pure culture is aided by heat shock (80 °C for 10 minutes) or alcohol shock that kills the vegetative cells in a mixed sample. Toxins identified by neutralisation in mice or by PCR. Classified into histotoxic (gas gangrene group: C. chauvoei, C. septicum, C. novyi, C. perfringens), neurotoxic (C. tetani, C. botulinum) and enteric groups."],
    ["Clostridium tetani: tetanus", "A slender Gram positive rod with a round terminal spore (drumstick or tennis racket appearance), motile, strict anaerobe, found in soil and the intestine of horses, cattle and humans. Spores enter wounds, especially deep puncture wounds with dead tissue, castration, docking, umbilical stumps, post-parturient uterus. Germination happens in anaerobic, necrotic tissue; the bacteria stay at the wound and release tetanospasmin (the toxin) and tetanolysin. The toxin travels up motor nerves (retrograde axonal transport) to the spinal cord and blocks release of glycine and GABA from inhibitory interneurons, causing spastic paralysis. Signs: stiff gait, prolapse of the third eyelid, flared nostrils, locked jaw (lockjaw, trismus), rigid 'saw horse' posture, hyperaesthesia and death from respiratory failure. Incubation 1 to 3 weeks or more. Horses and sheep are very susceptible; cattle, dogs and cats are more resistant; birds almost immune. Diagnosis is by signs and history (the organism is rarely isolated). Prevention: tetanus toxoid vaccination, tetanus antitoxin for wounded unvaccinated animals, wound care. Treatment is a clinical matter."],
    ["Clostridium botulinum: botulism", "A large, Gram positive, subterminal spore-forming anaerobic rod, found in soil and the intestine of animals; eight toxin types (A to G and H). Types C and D cause disease in cattle, sheep and birds (C also in ducks, chickens and mink), and B and C in horses (forage poisoning, shaker foal syndrome by toxicoinfection in foals). The toxin is made in decaying carcasses, spoiled silage and putrid feed, and is ingested preformed (intoxication). It blocks acetylcholine release at the neuromuscular junction, causing progressive flaccid paralysis, difficulty in chewing and swallowing, drooling, limb weakness and respiratory paralysis ('limberneck' in birds, 'loin disease' in cattle with pica and bone chewing in phosphorus-deficient pasture). Toxin detection by mouse inoculation and neutralisation, ELISA or PCR. Prevention: remove carcasses, correct mineral deficiencies, avoid feeding contaminated silage, vaccinate with toxoid in endemic areas. In humans, the toxin is a food poisoning agent."],
    ["Clostridium chauvoei: blackleg", "Gram positive rod with oval subterminal spores, motile, causes blackleg (black quarter) in cattle and buffalo of 6 months to 2 years old on pasture, and sometimes in sheep. Spores ingested are carried in macrophages to muscle and dormant until bruising or damage creates anaerobic conditions. Signs: sudden lameness, fever, hot painful swelling over the heavy muscle (hind quarter, shoulder), crepitus (gas) on palpation, black dry muscle with a rancid sweet smell, rapid death in 12 to 48 hours. Diagnosis: fluorescent antibody test or Gram stain of impression smears from muscle, culture, PCR; vaccinate calves with bacterin-toxoid (killed multicomponent vaccines), dispose of carcasses by burning or burial. Differentiate from anthrax (no bloat, blood clotting) and malignant oedema."],
    ["Clostridium septicum: malignant oedema", "Gram positive, motile rod, the cause of malignant oedema, gas gangrene and braxy (abomasitis in sheep). Wound contamination (shearing, castration, parturition, injection) leads to spreading oedematous, painful swellings with gas and a foul smell, toxaemia and death in a few days; no marked muscle involvement as in blackleg. Alpha toxin (a pore-forming haemolysin). Diagnosis is by smear showing long filamentous rods and the 'Gram stain of the liver surface' (long chains of rods over the liver capsule), culture, FAT. Prevention: vaccines (polyvalent), wound hygiene."],
    ["Clostridium novyi and C. haemolyticum", "C. novyi type B causes black disease (infectious necrotic hepatitis) in sheep, cattle and goats, after liver fluke migration creates anaerobic necrotic foci in which dormant spores in Kupffer cells germinate and produce alpha toxin, causing sudden death; skin is dark when removed (hence the black disease). C. novyi type A causes gas gangrene and big head in rams (fighting wounds). C. haemolyticum (C. novyi type D) causes bacillary haemoglobinuria (red water disease) in cattle and sheep, with beta toxin causing haemolysis, jaundice and red urine, also associated with liver fluke. Control: fluke control and vaccination."],
    ["Clostridium perfringens: types and diseases", "A large, non-motile, capsulated, encapsulated Gram positive rod with oval subterminal spores (spores are rarely seen in culture), rapid grower with a doubling time of 8 to 10 minutes, producing double zone haemolysis on blood agar and a stormy fermentation in litmus milk (the 'stormy clot') and positive Nagler reaction (lecithinase activity, inhibited by antiserum). Classified into toxinotypes A to E by production of the four major toxins: alpha (all types, phospholipase C, lecithinase), beta (types B and C), epsilon (types B and D), iota (type E); type F (enterotoxin, food poisoning) and G (necrotic enteritis in poultry, NetB toxin) have been added. Diseases: type A: gas gangrene, enteritis in calves and foals, food poisoning, necrotic enteritis in poultry (C. perfringens type A or G), haemolytic anaemia in calves and lambs; type B: lamb dysentery and enterotoxaemia in foals; type C: struck in adult sheep, haemorrhagic enteritis in piglets and neonatal calves; type D: pulpy kidney disease (overeating disease) in lambs and goats, due to epsilon toxin; type E: enterotoxaemia in calves and rabbits."],
    ["Enterotoxaemia: mechanism and control", "A sudden change to rich feed (grain, lush pasture) allows C. perfringens to overgrow in the small intestine, where it produces epsilon toxin (activated by trypsin), which enters the circulation and damages brain and kidney (pulpy kidney, post-mortem autolysis of the kidney, sudden death, convulsions, glycosuria). Vaccination with multicomponent clostridial toxoid vaccines (at 4 to 6 weeks, booster 4 to 6 weeks later, annual boosters, ewes before lambing for colostral protection) is the key to control, combined with careful feeding management."],
    ["Other clostridia", "C. difficile (antibiotic-associated colitis in horses, piglets, humans), C. colinum (ulcerative enteritis, quail disease), C. sordellii (abomasitis, sudden death in cattle and sheep), C. sporogenes (non-pathogenic putrefying anaerobe, spoilage and surrogate for sterilisation), C. spiroforme (iota toxin in rabbits). Vaccines are available for several multicomponent mixtures."],
    ["Laboratory diagnosis and practical points", "Take samples fresh (impression smears of the affected tissue, small intestine contents frozen for toxin tests; muscle for FAT; liver; blood). Direct smear with Gram stain showing large Gram positive rods with or without spores. Culture on blood agar in an anaerobic jar, in cooked meat medium and in thioglycollate. Biochemical tests: lecithinase (Nagler), lipase, gelatin liquefaction, fermentation of glucose, lactose and maltose, indole. PCR for toxin genes; ELISA for toxins; mouse toxin neutralisation test. Post-mortem changes rapidly obscure lesions, so examine animals soon after death."]
  ],
  tb: { h: ["Species", "Disease", "Host", "Toxin or key feature"], r: [["C. tetani", "Tetanus", "Horse, sheep, humans", "Tetanospasmin, spastic paralysis, drumstick spores"], ["C. botulinum", "Botulism", "Cattle, birds, horses, mink", "Neurotoxin types C and D, flaccid paralysis"], ["C. chauvoei", "Blackleg", "Cattle, sheep", "Gas in muscle, young animals on pasture"], ["C. septicum", "Malignant oedema, braxy", "Cattle, sheep", "Alpha toxin, wound infection"], ["C. novyi type B", "Black disease", "Sheep, cattle", "Alpha toxin after liver fluke damage"], ["C. haemolyticum", "Bacillary haemoglobinuria", "Cattle, sheep", "Beta toxin, haemolysis"], ["C. perfringens type D", "Pulpy kidney disease", "Lambs, kids", "Epsilon toxin"], ["C. perfringens type C", "Struck, haemorrhagic enteritis", "Sheep, piglets, calves", "Beta toxin"]] },
  df: ["Enterotoxaemia: disease caused by toxins produced by clostridia in the intestine and absorbed into the blood.", "Blackleg: acute clostridial myositis of young cattle caused by C. chauvoei.", "Nagler reaction: lecithinase activity of C. perfringens alpha toxin on egg yolk agar."],
  tm: [["Tetanospasmin", "Tetanus toxin"], ["Epsilon toxin", "Toxin of C. perfringens type D"], ["Pulpy kidney", "Overeating disease of lambs"], ["Braxy", "Abomasitis of sheep by C. septicum"], ["Big head", "Gas gangrene in rams by C. novyi A"], ["Stormy clot", "Gas production in litmus milk"], ["Cooked meat medium", "Anaerobic broth for clostridia"], ["Limberneck", "Botulism in birds"]],
  st: ["Spores are in soil or the gut.", "A wound, bruise, feed change or liver damage creates anaerobic conditions.", "Spores germinate and bacteria multiply.", "Toxins are released.", "The animal dies suddenly or shows nervous or muscle signs.", "Vaccination and management prevent it."],
  ex: ["A lamb dies suddenly after grazing lush pasture: pulpy kidney.", "A young bullock with a hot, crackling swelling of the hindquarter: blackleg.", "A horse with a stiff posture and prolapsed third eyelid after a nail wound: tetanus."],
  k: ["Spore position and shape.", "Toxins of each species.", "C. perfringens types.", "Blackleg vs malignant oedema vs anthrax.", "Vaccination schedule logic."],
  m: ["Tetanus and botulism are toxaemias, not bacteraemias. C. perfringens is non-motile. Boiling does not kill spores."],
  sa: [["Name the toxin of C. tetani and its effect.", "Tetanospasmin; blocks release of inhibitory neurotransmitters, causing spastic paralysis."], ["What causes black disease in sheep?", "Clostridium novyi type B after liver fluke damage."], ["What is pulpy kidney disease?", "Enterotoxaemia of lambs by C. perfringens type D epsilon toxin."]],
  la: [["Describe the clostridial diseases of livestock with their causative organisms and toxins.", "Tetanus, botulism, blackleg, malignant oedema, black disease, enterotoxaemia, features, control."], ["Describe the types of Clostridium perfringens and the diseases they cause.", "Toxinotypes A to E, alpha, beta, epsilon, iota, diseases, vaccines."]],
  q: [["Spastic paralysis is caused by", ["Tetanus toxin", "Botulinum toxin", "Anthrax toxin", "Epsilon toxin"], 0, "Blocks inhibitory neurotransmitters."],
    ["Black disease is associated with", ["Liver fluke", "Ticks", "Lungworm", "Mites"], 0, "Necrotic foci."],
    ["C. perfringens type D produces", ["Epsilon toxin", "Beta toxin", "Iota toxin", "Tetanospasmin"], 0, "Pulpy kidney."],
    ["Botulism is mostly", ["An intoxication", "A septicaemia", "A venereal disease", "A mycosis"], 0, "Preformed toxin."],
    ["C. chauvoei causes", ["Blackleg", "Tetanus", "Botulism", "Braxy"], 0, "In cattle."]],
  f: [["Tetanus", "Spastic paralysis by C. tetani"], ["Botulism", "Flaccid paralysis by toxin"], ["Blackleg", "C. chauvoei myositis"], ["Enterotoxaemia", "C. perfringens toxin disease"], ["Black disease", "C. novyi type B"]],
  d: ["Make a chart of clostridia, toxins and diseases.", "Study the multicomponent clostridial vaccine schedule.", "Practise anaerobic culture steps."],
});

topic("micro", "Actinomyces, Nocardia, Streptomyces and Dermatophilus", {
  o: ["Describe the characters of the branching filamentous bacteria", "Describe actinomycosis (lumpy jaw) and Actinomyces bovis", "Describe nocardiosis, actinomycetoma and bovine mastitis by Nocardia", "Describe Streptomyces and Dermatophilus congolensis (rain scald, lumpy wool)", "Describe diagnosis and control"],
  i: "This chapter groups the branching, filamentous Gram positive bacteria in the syllabus: Actinomyces, Nocardia, Streptomyces and Dermatophilus. They look like fungi and were once classified with them, but they are true bacteria, as shown by their size and antibiotic sensitivity. They cause chronic granulomatous disease, skin infections and mastitis.",
  s: "Imagine a bacterium that grows in thin branching threads like a fungus. These are the actinomycetes. Some live in the mouth and cause lumpy jaw when a wound lets them in. Others live in soil and cause infections of the skin and lungs when they enter a wound.",
  n: [
    ["General features of actinomycetes", "Gram positive, non-motile, non-sporing (Streptomyces forms conidia), branching filaments 0.5 to 1 micrometre wide (hyphae fragment into rods and cocci). Fungus-like colonies; many produce antibiotics (Streptomyces produces streptomycin, tetracyclines and others). Many are acid-fast to some degree (Nocardia: partially acid-fast with a weak acid decolouriser). They cause granulomatous or suppurative lesions with 'sulphur granules' (yellow, hard, gritty clumps of bacteria and host material) in the pus of actinomycosis and actinomycetoma."],
    ["Actinomyces bovis: lumpy jaw", "A Gram positive, non-acid-fast, pleomorphic, branching rod, anaerobic or microaerophilic (a normal inhabitant of the bovine mouth and pharynx). It enters through wounds from sharp feed (awns, stubble, bone) into the mandible, maxilla or soft tissue, causing a chronic granulomatous osteomyelitis, with a hard, immovable swelling of the jaw (lumpy jaw), draining sinuses, and pus with yellowish sulphur granules. Soft tissue lesions occur in the tongue (wooden tongue is by Actinobacillus lignieresii instead), pharynx, and subcutaneous tissue. Teeth are loosened, eating becomes difficult and the animal loses condition. In pigs, A. suis causes mastitis; in dogs, A. viscosus and A. canis cause pyothorax and abscesses. Diagnosis: crushed sulphur granules stained by Gram show a central Gram positive mass and peripheral Gram negative club-shaped rosettes; culture under anaerobic conditions; radiography. Prevention: avoid coarse, thorny feed and treat early."],
    ["Nocardia", "Nocardia asteroides complex (N. farcinica, N. nova, N. cyriacigeorgica), N. brasiliensis and N. otitidiscaviarum are aerobic, soil-borne, partially acid-fast branching filaments (acid-fast if decolourised with 1 per cent sulphuric acid). They grow on ordinary media (but slowly, in 3 to 7 days) with chalky, dry, wrinkled orange or white colonies and a musty or earthy smell. In animals: bovine mastitis (N. asteroides; acute or chronic granulomatous mastitis after intramammary infusions with contaminated products, poor response to treatment), abortion in cows, pneumonia and pleuritis in dogs and cats, and cutaneous nocardiosis and actinomycetoma (mycetoma) by N. brasiliensis in dogs and cats. Opportunistic infections occur in immunosuppressed animals, such as dogs with distemper, and in humans with AIDS. Diagnosis: smears from pus, acid-fast stain (modified), culture, PCR."],
    ["Streptomyces", "Soil saprophytes, aerobic, forming aerial mycelium and chains of spores (conidia). Streptomyces somaliensis and S. madurae cause actinomycetoma in humans and sometimes animals (a chronic granulomatous disease of the foot or limb with grains in sinus tracts). Their main importance is as the source of antibiotics (streptomycin, chloramphenicol, tetracyclines, erythromycin, neomycin, nystatin). They give soil its 'earthy' smell (geosmin)."],
    ["Dermatophilus congolensis", "A Gram positive, facultative anaerobic, branching filamentous organism that divides in transverse and longitudinal planes to form motile coccoid zoospores (a feature giving the 'railroad track' or parallel rows of cocci in smears). Colonies are small, yellowish, adherent to the agar, haemolytic, on blood agar in a candle jar. It causes dermatophilosis (streptothricosis): rain scald (cattle, horses), lumpy wool (sheep), strawberry foot rot (sheep), mycotic dermatitis of cattle, 'cutaneous streptothricosis' in cattle and buffalo in the tropics, and in humans (a rare zoonosis). Infection needs damage to the skin (prolonged wetting, ticks, biting flies, thorns); zoospores released in moisture invade the epidermis, and the neutrophils form crusts that are lifted off with hair, leaving a paint-brush pattern. Crusts and scabs contain the organism, which survives in them for years in dry conditions."],
    ["Clinical features and diagnosis of dermatophilosis", "Cattle: matted hair with thick crusts on the back, neck, and flanks, with pain, itching, and loss of production; heavy lesions in tick-infested zebu cattle in Africa (Amblyomma variegatum) and in India during the rains. Sheep: lumpy wool with crusts of wool and exudate; strawberry foot rot in the lower limbs. Horses: rain scald and mud fever of the pasterns. Diagnosis: Giemsa- or Gram-stained smear from a crust or scab impression, showing branching filaments segmented into parallel rows of coccoid cells (railroad tracks); culture on blood agar in 10 per cent CO2; PCR. Differential diagnosis includes dermatophytosis, mange and photosensitisation."],
    ["Control of dermatophilosis", "Reduce wetting (shelter), control ticks and biting flies, avoid skin abrasion, isolate cases, treat under veterinary advice and shear carefully, dip or spray with approved products, dry housing, and good nutrition. Crusts should be burned as they carry the infection."],
    ["Other actinomycetes in brief", "Rhodococcus (see earlier chapter), Actinomyces hordeovulneris (dogs, grass awns), Actinobacillus (Gram negative, not an actinomycete despite the name: Actinobacillus lignieresii causes wooden tongue; see a later chapter), and Streptomyces griseus (antibiotic producer). Actinomyces israelii is the human actinomycosis agent."],
    ["Differentiation of the filamentous bacteria", "Actinomyces: anaerobic or microaerophilic, non-acid-fast, sulphur granules. Nocardia: aerobic, partially acid-fast, orange colonies, soil. Streptomyces: aerobic, not acid-fast, aerial spore chains, antibiotic production. Dermatophilus: facultative, zoospores, railroad-track cocci, crust on skin. Sulphur granule appearance and the acid-fast reaction are the quick tests."]
  ],
  tb: { h: ["Genus", "Oxygen", "Acid fast", "Disease", "Key feature"], r: [["Actinomyces bovis", "Anaerobic or microaerophilic", "No", "Lumpy jaw in cattle", "Sulphur granules, osteomyelitis"], ["Nocardia", "Aerobic", "Partial", "Mastitis, pneumonia, mycetoma", "Soil organism, orange colonies"], ["Streptomyces", "Aerobic", "No", "Rare mycetoma", "Antibiotic producers"], ["Dermatophilus congolensis", "Facultative", "No", "Rain scald, lumpy wool, strawberry foot rot", "Zoospores, railroad tracks"]] },
  df: ["Sulphur granule: a yellow granule of bacterial filaments and host material in the pus of actinomycosis.", "Dermatophilosis: skin infection caused by Dermatophilus congolensis.", "Actinomycetoma: a chronic granulomatous infection with grains caused by filamentous bacteria."],
  tm: [["Lumpy jaw", "Actinomycosis of the jaw of cattle"], ["Wooden tongue", "Actinobacillosis of the tongue"], ["Railroad tracks", "Parallel cocci rows of Dermatophilus"], ["Zoospore", "Motile coccoid stage"], ["Hypha", "Filament"], ["Rain scald", "Dermatophilosis of the back in horses and cattle"], ["Lumpy wool", "Dermatophilosis in sheep"], ["Mycetoma", "Chronic granuloma with grains"]],
  st: ["The organism enters through a wound or damaged skin.", "A chronic granuloma or crusting dermatitis forms.", "Pus or crusts contain filaments or granules.", "Smear or culture shows branching filaments.", "Treatment and management follow the diagnosis.", "Prevention reduces injury, wetness and vectors."],
  ex: ["A cow with a hard swelling on the mandible and draining sinuses: lumpy jaw.", "A sheep with wool matted into crusts after heavy rains: dermatophilosis.", "A dog with pleural effusion and a partially acid-fast filamentous organism: Nocardia."],
  k: ["Branching Gram positive filaments.", "Sulphur granules.", "Nocardia partial acid-fastness.", "Dermatophilus zoospores.", "Lumpy jaw vs wooden tongue."],
  m: ["Actinomyces bovis is in the bovine mouth as a normal flora. Actinobacillus lignieresii is Gram negative. Nocardia is soil-borne and partially acid-fast."],
  sa: [["What causes lumpy jaw in cattle?", "Actinomyces bovis."], ["What is seen in a smear of Dermatophilus crusts?", "Branching filaments divided into parallel rows of cocci (railroad tracks)."], ["What is special about Streptomyces?", "It is the main source of antibiotics."]],
  la: [["Describe actinomycosis in cattle and Nocardia infections of animals.", "A. bovis, lumpy jaw, sulphur granules, Nocardia mastitis, mycetoma, diagnosis, control."], ["Describe Dermatophilus congolensis and the diseases it causes.", "Morphology, zoospores, rain scald, lumpy wool, strawberry foot rot, diagnosis, control."]],
  q: [["Sulphur granules are seen in", ["Actinomycosis", "Anthrax", "Tetanus", "Listeriosis"], 0, "Pus."],
    ["Nocardia is", ["Partially acid-fast", "Fully acid-fast", "Gram negative", "Anaerobic only"], 0, "Weak acid decolourisation."],
    ["Dermatophilus has", ["Zoospores", "Capsule only", "Endospores", "Flagella only"], 0, "Motile cocci."],
    ["Streptomyces is important as a", ["Source of antibiotics", "Zoonotic pathogen only", "Virus", "Parasite"], 0, "Soil organism."],
    ["Lumpy jaw affects the", ["Mandible", "Liver", "Lung only", "Udder"], 0, "Osteomyelitis."]],
  f: [["Actinomyces", "Lumpy jaw bacterium"], ["Nocardia", "Partially acid-fast soil actinomycete"], ["Dermatophilus", "Rain scald bacterium"], ["Streptomyces", "Antibiotic-producing genus"], ["Sulphur granule", "Colony in pus"]],
  d: ["Crush a sulphur granule and Gram stain it (demonstration).", "Make a table for the four genera.", "Study rain scald prevention for horses."],
});
