topic("micro", "Practical Guide 3: Virology Laboratory (Samples, Isolation, Cell Culture, CPE, Titration and Serology)", {
  o: ["Describe the orientation to a virology laboratory and the biosafety rules", "Describe sample collection and transport for virus isolation", "Describe isolation in animals, embryonated eggs and cell culture", "Describe CPE, TCID50, plaque assay and neutralisation tests", "Describe NDV HA and HI, ELISA and molecular diagnosis"],
  i: "Unit 5 practicals require a firm orientation to biosafety, sample handling, virus isolation, cell culture, titration and serology. The routine is long and every step is sensitive to contamination, so this guide arranges the work into the order in which you will perform it.",
  s: "In virology, the sample is usually the weakest link. A swab left in the sun is worth nothing, however good the lab. Keep samples cold, in the right medium, and ship them fast with complete information.",
  n: [
    ["Orientation and biosafety", "Know the layout: sample reception, preparation room, cell culture room (clean, with laminar flow cabinet and CO2 incubators), media preparation, molecular lab (separate pre-PCR and post-PCR rooms), serology room, washing and autoclave room. Wear a gown and gloves, change gloves between tasks, wash hands, and keep doors closed. Use class II biosafety cabinets for virus work, with 70 per cent ethanol for surface decontamination and appropriate chemical disinfectants (sodium hypochlorite) for spills. Do not carry cell culture materials into non-sterile areas. Waste is autoclaved before disposal and sharps are collected in puncture-proof containers. Biological safety levels apply to high-risk viruses (avian influenza, rabies, FMD)."],
    ["Sample collection, transport and processing", "Select samples by the disease: nasal, oropharyngeal and cloacal or faecal swabs (NDV, influenza, IBV), tracheal swab, vesicular epithelium and fluid (FMD), brain (rabies, CDV, Aujeszky's: half in 50 per cent glycerol saline and half in formalin), blood with EDTA (viraemia: BTV, EIA, BVDV) and serum (serology), spleen, liver and lung (septicaemic viruses), skin scabs (pox, orf), ear notch (BVDV), bursa (IBD), and faeces (rotavirus, parvovirus, coronavirus). Place swabs in viral transport medium (VTM: Hank's buffered salt solution or tryptose phosphate broth with antibiotics and 0.5 to 2 per cent protein such as bovine serum albumin or gelatin), keep at 4 °C (not above) and deliver within 24 to 48 hours (longer storage at -70 °C or in liquid nitrogen; avoid repeated freeze-thaw; for FMD use phosphate buffered glycerol). Label with details and fill a submission form (species, age, vaccination history, clinical signs, number sick and dead, date and place). Processing: grind tissue as 10 to 20 per cent suspension in transport medium with sterile sand, centrifuge at 2000 to 3000 rpm for 10 to 15 minutes, filter through 0.45 micrometre filter or add antibiotics (penicillin, streptomycin, gentamicin, amphotericin B) for 30 to 60 minutes at room temperature to remove bacteria, then inoculate."],
    ["Virus isolation in embryonated eggs", "Incubate fertile eggs (SPF) at 37.5 °C with 60 to 70 per cent humidity and turning; candle eggs on day 9 to 11 and mark the air sac, the embryo, and a spot on the allantoic membrane. Disinfect the shell with iodine and alcohol, make a small hole over the air space with a sterilised punch, and inject 0.1 to 0.2 ml of inoculum into the allantoic cavity (NDV, influenza, IBV) using a 1 ml syringe with 25 gauge needle through the hole at an angle of about 45 degrees; for CAM inoculation (pox viruses, ILTV), drop the CAM by making a window; yolk sac inoculation of 5 to 7 day embryos; seal the hole with wax, and incubate for 3 to 5 days with daily candling (discard deaths in the first 24 hours as non-specific). Chill eggs at 4 °C for several hours or overnight (to prevent bleeding), harvest the allantoic fluid with a sterile pipette, and test by the HA test. Examine embryos for lesions: dwarfing, haemorrhages, and pocks on the CAM. Dispose of eggs and fluids by autoclaving or soaking in disinfectant."],
    ["Cell culture: maintenance and virus isolation", "Revive cells from liquid nitrogen by rapid thawing at 37 °C, dilution in warm growth medium, centrifugation, and seeding in flasks. Subculture (passage) a confluent monolayer: remove medium, wash with PBS, add trypsin-EDTA, incubate 2 to 5 minutes until cells detach, neutralise with serum medium, count cells with a haemocytometer and trypan blue (live cells exclude the dye), and seed at 1:3 to 1:5 ratio. Growth medium: MEM or DMEM with 5 to 10 per cent foetal bovine serum, antibiotics (penicillin and streptomycin), and amphotericin B; maintenance medium with 2 per cent serum. For virus isolation, grow cells in tubes, 24-well plates or flasks to 70 to 90 per cent confluence, discard medium, inoculate the filtered sample, adsorb for 1 hour at 37 °C with occasional shaking, add maintenance medium, and incubate at 37 °C with 5 per cent CO2; examine daily for CPE (rounding, clumping, granulation, syncytia, vacuolation, detachment); blind passages are done when no CPE appears at 5 to 7 days. Cell lines in use: Vero, BHK-21, MDBK, PK-15, CEF, MDCK, and RK-13. Contamination control: use aseptic technique, test for mycoplasma regularly, and discard contaminated cultures."],
    ["Titration: TCID50 and plaque assay", "TCID50: make ten-fold serial dilutions of virus (10 to the power minus 1 to minus 10); inoculate each dilution into replicate wells (e.g., 4 or 8) of a 96 well plate with cells; incubate for 3 to 7 days and read the number of wells with CPE for each dilution; calculate the 50 per cent endpoint by Reed and Muench or Spearman-Karber and express as TCID50 per ml. EID50 for eggs follows the same principle. Plaque assay: infect confluent monolayers in 6 well plates with serial dilutions of virus, adsorb 1 hour, overlay with 1 per cent agarose or methylcellulose in maintenance medium, incubate for 2 to 5 days, fix with formalin, stain with crystal violet or neutral red, count plaques in wells having 10 to 100 plaques, and calculate PFU per ml (number of plaques divided by dilution factor and volume). Plaque purification for cloning of virus strain: pick isolated plaques."],
    ["Neutralisation tests and serology", "Serum neutralisation test (SNT): inactivate serum at 56 °C for 30 minutes, make two-fold dilutions in a 96-well plate, mix with 100 TCID50 of virus per well, incubate for 1 hour, add cell suspension, incubate and read for CPE after 3 to 5 days; the serum titre is the highest dilution with complete neutralisation; include virus back-titration, cell, serum toxicity and reference serum controls. AGID for IBD, avian influenza, and Marek's (antigen and positive serum; read precipitin lines). NDV HA and HI as described earlier. Antigen detection ELISA and antibody ELISA kits for FMD, PPR, IBD, AI (follow the kit protocol exactly; use the kit's controls and calculate percent inhibition or S/P ratio). Fluorescent antibody test (rabies): touch impressions of brain smear, fix in cold acetone for 30 minutes, add FITC-conjugated antirabies globulin, incubate at 37 °C for 30 minutes in humid chamber, wash with PBS, mount in buffered glycerol, and view under fluorescence microscope looking for apple-green fluorescent granules."],
    ["Molecular diagnosis", "RNA extraction (TRIzol or spin column kit), cDNA synthesis by reverse transcriptase with random hexamers or specific primers, PCR master mix, thermal cycling programme, gel electrophoresis of amplicons for the expected product size, with positive control, negative control (water) and internal control. Real-time RT-PCR: probe-based or SYBR green, reading Ct values (interpretation: low Ct means high viral load; no Ct is negative; follow kit-specific cut-offs). Sequencing for confirmation and phylogeny. Strict anti-contamination rules: separate rooms and dedicated pipettes for reagent preparation, sample addition and amplification, use of filter tips, regular decontamination with 10 per cent bleach or DNA-away reagents, and run no-template controls with each batch."],
    ["Reporting, quality and ethics", "Record the details of each sample in a register, give a unique laboratory number, report results with a timeline, quote the method, mention the limit of the test, and the interpretation (positive, negative, inconclusive), and send notifiable disease results to the authority. Quality: standard operating procedures, calibration of equipment (pipettes, incubators, balances), proficiency testing and external quality assessment, reagent traceability, and good laboratory practice. Ethics: use animals only when needed, with committee approval, and observe confidentiality for farm data."],
    ["Quick checklists", "Egg inoculation: candle, mark, disinfect, punch, inject, seal, incubate, chill, harvest, HA. Cell culture isolation: process sample, filter, adsorb, maintenance medium, observe CPE daily, blind passage, confirm by immunostaining or PCR. Titration: serial dilution, replicates, incubation, read, calculate. FAT: impression, fix, stain, wash, read. Remember to include controls in every step, and log the temperature and humidity records of incubators."]
  ],
  tb: { h: ["Practical", "Sample or system", "Read-out", "Typical virus"], r: [["Egg inoculation", "9 to 11 day SPF egg, allantoic cavity", "HA of allantoic fluid, embryo death", "NDV, influenza, IBV"], ["Cell culture isolation", "Vero, BHK-21, MDBK, PK-15", "CPE", "FMDV, rabies, BHV-1, CSFV"], ["TCID50", "96-well plate dilutions", "Endpoint by Reed-Muench", "Any CPE-producing virus"], ["Plaque assay", "Agarose overlay", "Plaque count (PFU/ml)", "Herpes, pox, rhabdo"], ["SNT", "Serum dilutions plus 100 TCID50", "Absence of CPE", "Antibody to PPR, BVD, FMD"], ["FAT", "Brain impression smear", "Apple-green fluorescence", "Rabies, CDV"], ["RT-PCR", "RNA extract", "Band or Ct value", "NDV, AI, PPR, FMD"]] },
  df: ["VTM: viral transport medium used to keep virus viable in swabs.", "Blind passage: serial passage of a sample in cell culture without visible CPE.", "S/P ratio: sample to positive ratio in ELISA."],
  tm: [["Candling", "Examining eggs with light"], ["Adsorption", "Attachment of virus to cells"], ["Confluence", "Percentage of surface covered by cells"], ["Trypsinisation", "Detaching cells with trypsin"], ["Overlay", "Semi-solid medium to restrict spread of virus"], ["Back titration", "Titration of virus used in the test"], ["Ct value", "Cycle threshold in real-time PCR"], ["Haemocytometer", "Counting chamber for cells"]],
  st: ["The sample is collected in VTM and kept cold.", "It is processed and filtered.", "It is inoculated into eggs or cells.", "CPE or HA shows growth.", "The virus is identified by serology or PCR.", "Titres and reports are recorded."],
  ex: ["A swab from a chicken with respiratory signs is inoculated into eggs and HA positive fluid is obtained.", "A brain impression shows fluorescence.", "A virus dilution series gives a TCID50 titre."],
  k: ["VTM and cold chain.", "Egg routes.", "Cell culture steps.", "Reed-Muench.", "FAT procedure."],
  m: ["Discard embryo deaths in the first 24 hours. Plaque counts are done in wells with 10 to 100 plaques. Contamination control is a core concept."],
  sa: [["What is VTM?", "A buffered medium with protein and antibiotics used to preserve virus in swabs."], ["What is the difference between TCID50 and PFU?", "TCID50 is the dose infecting 50 per cent of cultures, PFU is the number of plaque forming units counted."], ["How is rabies diagnosed by FAT?", "Brain impression smears are fixed in acetone and stained with FITC-labelled antirabies globulin, then viewed under fluorescence."]],
  la: [["Describe the isolation of viruses in embryonated eggs and cell culture.", "Sample processing, routes, incubation, harvest, CPE, identification."], ["Describe the titration of viruses by TCID50 and plaque assay and the serum neutralisation test.", "Dilutions, replicates, incubation, reading, calculation, controls."]],
  q: [["VTM is stored at", ["4 °C for short term", "37 °C", "60 °C", "Room temperature in sun"], 0, "Cold chain."],
    ["Allantoic cavity inoculation suits", ["NDV and influenza", "Pox virus only", "Rabies virus", "FMDV"], 0, "Egg route."],
    ["Plaque assay gives", ["PFU per ml", "CFU per ml", "Colonies", "Haemolysis units"], 0, "Infectivity."],
    ["FAT for rabies uses", ["Brain impression smear", "Serum", "Urine", "Faeces"], 0, "FITC conjugate."],
    ["TCID50 is calculated by", ["Reed and Muench method", "Gram method", "Lowry method", "Bradford method"], 0, "Endpoint dilution."]],
  f: [["VTM", "Viral transport medium"], ["CPE", "Cytopathic effect"], ["TCID50", "50 per cent tissue culture infective dose"], ["PFU", "Plaque forming unit"], ["SNT", "Serum neutralisation test"]],
  d: ["Rehearse the egg inoculation steps with a model.", "Practise Reed-Muench calculations.", "Make a sample submission form for a poultry outbreak."],
});
